  <h1 class="research-title">The Rise and Fall Race</h1>
  <p class="ref-lede">Pick up to four states and empires and watch them rise and fall, year by year: who led the world&#8217;s stage, when they overtook each other, and how long each held on.</p>
</header>

<section class="rc" id="race" aria-label="The race"><noscript><p class="pass-note">The race needs JavaScript; the <a href="/explorer">Empire Explorer</a> shows the same curves.</p></noscript></section>

<section class="rc-how" aria-labelledby="rc-how-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="rc-how-t">How the race is scored</h2>
  <p>Every state and empire profile has a power curve: the Institute&#8217;s judgement of the state&#8217;s strength through its history, from the works cited in its profile, on a scale of 0 to 10 that is scaled to its own peak. Alone, that cannot tell a great empire from a small kingdom: both reach 10. So the race weighs each curve by the state&#8217;s weight in the Institute&#8217;s analysis, the mean of its military power, territorial reach, economic strength and historical influence, also out of 10. A state&#8217;s standing in a year is its curve there times its weight, divided by ten. The same weight ranks the great powers on the century pages.</p>
  <p>These are interpretive judgements, not measurements: read a lead of a point or two as close. The curves and the scores are free to download as <a href="/dataset">open data</a>, and the <a href="/explorer">Empire Explorer</a> draws every curve side by side.</p>
